   "text": {
    "L1": "Mixtures can be separated by choosing a method for the materials inside them.",
    "L2": "First, leave muddy water undisturbed. Heavier insoluble particles settle at the bottom; this is sedimentation.",
    "L3": "Now gently pour off the clearer liquid above the settled layer. This is decantation.",
    "L4": "Filtration passes the mixture through cloth, paper, or a strainer. Insoluble solids stay behind, while the liquid passes through.",
    "L5": "But dissolved salt passes through a filter with the water. So filtering salt water does not remove its salt.",
    "L6": "To get the salt back, leave the salt water in the sun. The water evaporates and salt crystals remain.",
    "L7": "For sand, salt, and water, use sedimentation, decantation, filtration, then evaporation.",
    "L8": "Clear-looking water may still contain germs or dissolved substances. It needs boiling or purifying before drinking."
   },
